output.text:
Looking at the Hexi library's `block_allocator`, I need to understand how it manages its free list internally and demonstrate this behavior through the public `allocate()` and `deallocate()` methods.

Key observations:
1. `push()` adds a block to the head of the free list (LIFO structure)
2. `pop()` removes and returns the head block
3. `allocate()` calls `pop()` to get a free block
4. `deallocate()` calls `push()` to return a block to the free list
5. The allocator initially has all blocks in the free list

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/allocators/block_allocator.h>
#include <cstdint>
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>

// Note: push() and pop() are private, but we can observe their behavior
// through allocate() and deallocate() operations
template<size_t element_count = 10>
void demonstrate_block_allocator_behavior() {
    // Create a block_allocator for integers with element_count blocks
    hexi::block_allocator<int, element_count> allocator;

    std::cout << "=== Demonstrating Block Allocator Free List Behavior ===" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Block allocator created with " << element_count << " pre-allocated blocks" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Initially, all blocks are in the free list (managed by push/pop)" << std::endl << std::endl;

    // Phase 1: Allocate all blocks (this will call pop() internally for each allocation)
    std::cout << "Phase 1: Allocating blocks (each allocation calls pop() internally)" << std::endl;
    std::vector<int*> allocated_blocks;

    for(size_t i = 0; i < element_count; ++i) {
        int* block = allocator.allocate(static_cast<int>(i * 10));
        allocated_blocks.push_back(block);
        std::cout << "  Allocated block " << i << " with value " << *block 
                  << " (pop() removed block from free list head)" << std::endl;
    }

    std::cout << "\nAll " << element_count << " blocks allocated. Free list is now empty." << std::endl;
    std::cout << "Next allocation would trigger new allocation from heap." << std::endl << std::endl;

    // Phase 2: Deallocate some blocks (this will call push() internally)
    std::cout << "Phase 2: Deallocating blocks in LIFO order (each deallocation calls push())" << std::endl;

    // Deallocate blocks 2, 5, and 8 to demonstrate push() behavior
    std::vector<size_t> indices_to_free = {2, 5, 8};
    for(size_t idx : indices_to_free) {
        if(idx < allocated_blocks.size()) {
            std::cout << "  Deallocating block " << idx << " (push() adds to free list head)" << std::endl;
            allocator.deallocate(allocated_blocks[idx]);
            allocated_blocks[idx] = nullptr;
        }
    }

    std::cout << "\nFree list now contains 3 blocks in order: 8 -> 5 -> 2 (LIFO)" << std::endl << std::endl;

    // Phase 3: Reallocate to show LIFO behavior
    std::cout << "Phase 3: Reallocating blocks (demonstrates LIFO behavior of pop())" << std::endl;

    int* block_a = allocator.allocate(100);
    std::cout << "  Allocated new block A with value 100 (pop() returned block 8)" << std::endl;

    int* block_b = allocator.allocate(200);
    std::cout << "  Allocated new block B with value 200 (pop() returned block 5)" << std::endl;

    int* block_c = allocator.allocate(300);
    std::cout << "  Allocated new block C with value 300 (pop() returned block 2)" << std::endl;

    std::cout << "\nFree list is empty again." << std::endl << std::endl;

    // Phase 4: Clean up
    std::cout << "Phase 4: Cleaning up all allocations" << std::endl;
    allocator.deallocate(block_a);
    allocator.deallocate(block_b);
    allocator.deallocate(block_c);

    for(size_t i = 0; i < allocated_blocks.size(); ++i) {
        if(allocated_blocks[i] != nullptr) {
            allocator.deallocate(allocated_blocks[i]);
        }
    }

    std::cout << "All blocks returned to free list via push() operations" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "\n=== Summary ===" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "- push() adds freed blocks to the HEAD of the free list" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "- pop() removes and returns blocks from the HEAD of the free list" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "- This implements a LIFO (Last-In-First-Out) free list structure" << std::endl;
    std::cout << "- Benefits: O(1) allocation and deallocation performance" << std::endl;
}
```

This implementation demonstrates:

1. **Initial state**: All blocks are in the free list after `initialise_free_list()` calls `push()` for each block
2. **Allocation**: Each `allocate()` calls `pop()` to remove a block from the free list head
3. **Deallocation**: Each `deallocate()` calls `push()` to add the block back to the free list head
4. **LIFO behavior**: The last deallocated block is the first one returned on the next allocation
5. **Performance**: Both operations are O(1) since they only manipulate the head pointer

