Looking at the `static_buffer` API from the Hexi library, I can see it provides:
- `write(const void* source, size_type length)` - writes data to the buffer
- `read(void* destination, size_type length)` - reads data from the buffer
- `size()` - returns the number of bytes available to read

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/static_buffer.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

template<size_t buffer_size = 1024>
void write_data_to_buffer(hexi::static_buffer<std::byte, buffer_size>& buffer, const std::vector<uint8_t>& data) {
    // Write the vector data to the static_buffer
    // The write() method takes a pointer to the data and the length
    buffer.write(data.data(), data.size());
}

template<size_t buffer_size = 1024>
std::vector<uint8_t> read_data_from_buffer(hexi::static_buffer<std::byte, buffer_size>& buffer, size_t bytes_to_read) {
    // Determine the actual number of bytes to read
    // We cannot read more than what's available in the buffer
    size_t actual_bytes = std::min(bytes_to_read, buffer.size());

    // Create a vector to hold the read data
    std::vector<uint8_t> result(actual_bytes);

    // Read data from the buffer into the vector
    // The read() method takes a pointer to the destination and the length
    if (actual_bytes > 0) {
        buffer.read(result.data(), actual_bytes);
    }

    return result;
}
```

**Explanation:**

1. **`write_data_to_buffer`**: 
   - Uses `buffer.write(data.data(), data.size())` to write the entire contents of the vector to the static_buffer
   - `data.data()` provides a pointer to the underlying array of the vector
   - `data.size()` provides the number of elements to write

2. **`read_data_from_buffer`**:
   - First determines the actual number of bytes to read using `std::min(bytes_to_read, buffer.size())` to avoid reading more data than available
   - Creates a result vector with the appropriate size
   - Uses `buffer.read(result.data(), actual_bytes)` to read data from the static_buffer into the vector
   - The read operation advances the read cursor in the buffer
   - Returns the vector containing the read data

