Looking at the reference code, I can see that:

1. For `binary_stream_writer`, the `operator<<` with `std::string` or `std::string_view` uses the `prefixed` format by default
2. For `binary_stream_reader`, the `operator>>` with `std::string` uses the `prefixed` format by default

The prefixed format means the string length is written/read as a 4-byte little-endian integer before the actual string data.

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_writer.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/binary_stream_reader.h>
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <string>

void write_string_to_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_writer& writer, const std::string& str) {
    // Write string using prefixed format (length prefix + data)
    writer << str;
}

std::string read_string_from_stream(hexi::pmc::binary_stream_reader& reader) {
    // Read string using prefixed format (length prefix + data)
    std::string result;
    reader >> result;
    return result;
}
```

The implementation is straightforward because:

1. **`write_string_to_stream`**: The `binary_stream_writer::operator<<(const std::string&)` automatically uses the prefixed format, which writes a 4-byte length prefix followed by the string data.

2. **`read_string_from_stream`**: The `binary_stream_reader::operator>>(std::string&)` automatically reads the prefixed format, which reads the 4-byte length prefix and then reads that many bytes into the string.

Both operations handle the prefixed format internally, so we just need to use the stream operators directly.

