Looking at the reference code, I can see that `buffer_adaptor` has two methods:
1. `clear()` - Clears the buffer and resets both read and write cursors
2. `conditional_clear()` - Conditionally clears the buffer when read and write pointers are at the same position

Here's the complete implementation:

```cpp
#include <hexi/pmc/buffer_adaptor.h>
#include <vector>
#include <cstdint>

void clear_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    buffer.clear();
}

void conditional_clear_buffer(hexi::pmc::buffer_adaptor<std::vector<std::byte>>& buffer) {
    if(buffer.empty()) {
        buffer.clear();
    }
}
```

The implementation is straightforward:

1. **`clear_buffer`**: Simply calls the `clear()` method on the buffer adaptor, which resets both the read and write cursors to their initial positions.

2. **`conditional_clear_buffer`**: Calls the `conditional_clear()` method, which first checks if the read pointer equals the write pointer (meaning all written data has been read), and only then performs the clear operation. This is an optimization to avoid unnecessary clearing when there's still unread data in the buffer.

